      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "This is a classic architectural trade-off in LLM serving: **Model Parallelism (Tensor Parallelism)** vs. **Data Parallelism (Replication)**.\n\nBelow is a detailed comparison of **Option A: Tensor Parallelism (TP=4)** across four GPUs versus **Option B: Four Independent Replicas (TP=1)** behind a load bal/router.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Latency\n\n| Metric | Option A: TP=4 (Single Model) | Option B: 4x Replicas (TP=1) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Per-Request Latency** | **Higher.** Each token generation requires synchronization across all 4 GPUs. The latency is bounded by the slowest GPU and the communication overhead. | **Lower.** Each request is handled by a single GPU. No inter-GPU synchronization is needed during inference. |\n| **Tail Latency (P99)** | **Worse.** Synchronization barriers (e.g., `all-reduce`) can cause jitter if one GPU is slightly slower or if PCIe/NVLink bandwidth fluctuates. | **Better.** More predictable. If one replica is slow, it only affects its own queue, not the entire system. |\n| **First Token Latency (TTFT)** | **Higher.** Prefill phase involves splitting the matrix multiplication across GPUs, requiring communication at every layer. | **Lower.** Prefill happens entirely on one GPU. |\n\n**Verdict:** **Option B (Replicas)** wins for latency-sensitive applications (e.g., chatbots, real-time agents).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Throughput\n\n| Metric | Option A: TP=4 (Single Model) | Option B: 4x Replicas (TP=1) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Max Throughput** | **Lower.** Throughput is limited by the communication overhead. As batch size increases, the `all-reduce` operations become a bottleneck. | **Higher.** Throughput scales nearly linearly with the number of replicas (assuming no CPU/network bottleneck). Each GPU works independently. |\n| **Batching Efficiency** | **Good for large batches.** TP allows serving a very large model that wouldn’t fit on one GPU. | **Limited by VRAM.** Each replica can only handle a batch size that fits in its own VRAM. |\n\n**Verdict:** **Option B (Replicas)** wins for high-through"
      },
      "finish_reason": "length"
    }
